      // Scope-aware connector proofs retain every legacy tool name and saved grant across upgrade and renewal.
      // Capacity refusals remain typed across every deploy transport and both web error maps.
      // A timed-out compile replies after its stalled work phase, release and error records;
      // diagnostics wait for that correlated terminal reply before checking the complete trail.
      // Dependency work may resume after bundling; only its last phase and the terminal tail are fixed.
      // Deferred management-app setup sends the onboarding POST before waiting for committed metadata and the user's link and checking inventory once, because GET requests skip dispatch and would leave setup waiting for the recovery cron.
      // Physical database proofs await committed pool accounts and links before seeding synthetic pool metadata.
      // Provisioning readiness wakes on app, account and link commits, not only setup metadata changes.
      description "Main push Checks may reuse only a completed successful batch push of the same workflow in the same repository whose Git tree and workflow blob equal the candidate. Selection decides reuse before anything else, and a reused Check selects nothing. The aggregate binds the retained run and head evidence to its checked-out main commit and requires the main-push dependency history guard and the jobs that start with the Check to pass while every other suite job skips; all lookup failures run the full suite. Deploy and manual main release still require the exact candidate's successful push Check. Native gateway updates regenerate the plugin module graph in isolation through the Go checksum database, clearing inherited checksum exemptions and workspace flags before publishing dependency metadata. Catalog OAuth proof compares the complete public authorization-server document with its reviewed digest. Unavailable or undecodable documents fail separately from digest changes and retain the affected app, method, metadata URL and closed failure reason. A changed digest refuses on its first complete response and reports expected and observed hashes in logs and the job summary. A hash-matching document with invalid protocol fields refuses with every failing property and its expected and observed values. The reviewed audio-service document adds user and pronunciation-dictionary read scopes only; issuer, endpoints, client authentication, grants and PKCE remain unchanged, and advertised scopes do not expand connector consent. Catalog source checks reuse target-branch provenance offline only for unchanged immutable repository pins, exact allowlists and snapshot/file hashes. Changed pins or hashes and missing target evidence require authenticated outside-service verification without rewriting the candidate. Explicit source refreshes record new verified provenance. Source GET reads use bounded backoff and server retry delays, including body transport failures. Failed transport or exhausted retries never become evidence of source drift or successful verification. Refusals name changed paths and hashes for every blocked package in logs and the job summary. Pull-request and batch comparisons fetch the current base branch and bind selection and dependency guards to its merge-base with the candidate. Event snapshots and stale tracking refs cannot reintroduce merged compatibility changes. Ordinary main pushes retain their before-commit comparison. The dependency guard resolves its boundary from the checked-out merge commit; affected-file selection retains the PR-head comparison. HITL browser request observers own only the current document's requests; a held older request cannot delay a later document or hide a current failure. Dashboard batch profiling prepares the complete E2E assets for each source variant before measuring it, so both variants satisfy the same strict web freshness check. Pool recipe journeys run in the pool CI gate and retain their completed checks and first failure. Pool import fixtures build the production packager's complete source files, including relocated shared host dependencies, instead of maintaining a separate directory copy. Adopted account, credential and MCP scenarios preserve every scheduled managed target. The audited self-host account cohort runs in its own selected job so the original host-managed job returns to its prior load; both keep the same preparation, evidence retention and 20-minute limit. Bulk upload acknowledgement proofs run once in the account runtime job, apart from retained pool usage proofs, with the same self-host target, one worker, evidence retention and twenty-minute job limit. Local ownership stays unchanged. Managed Cloud account groups split between transport/telemetry and policy/listed actions jobs, preserving all 44 scenarios, isolated one-worker runs, evidence and 20-minute deadlines; both jobs are required by the aggregate. Attached Cloud targets remain explicit manual declarations with reasons and commands beside their self-host CI counterparts. The fast gate inventories prepared E2E test calls and standalone acceptance scripts against declared groups, real target selection and executable workflow steps. Existing unselected tests are frozen in one reviewed exception file by exact identity and source plus plan fingerprint. New or changed omissions require CI wiring or a reviewed exception with a reason; an exception fails once CI selects it. Managed Cloud pool proofs inject their receiver through the existing composition-root poolOrigin, require exactly one origin declaration and one access-lifecycle wiring in each producer, and restore both saved source files on success or failure. Hook and receiver implementations remain unchanged. Completed proof exports preserve environment and runtime preboot, launch and switch receipts before pruning, so their caller can verify framework reuse and runtime identity. Every E2E upload collects those retained exports, falling back to unpruned original reports when retention is interrupted. Preparation removes nonignored untracked source only within its pinned upstream checkout before replaying the current carry set. One lock per checkout covers its reset, carry replay and recording; the patched tree is built from the carries applied to the pin's index and recorded, only when the replayed worktree matches it exactly, as a commit whose only parent is the pin and whose message names a key binding the pin, every carry's name and bytes in order, the replay code, Git's version, configuration, attribute files and environment, so a hit checks that commit shape and key before restoring, only local preparations and the Check workflow read recorded trees because code under test shares the cache, any failed check replays the carries instead, and collection keeps each cache repository's 32 most recently used trees. The checkout must be its own Git worktree root and cannot be a symbolic link; ignored dependency and build output, including web freshness stamps, survive. Check omits bundled version-control and desktop-runtime lifecycle downloads from its private dependency attempts because only upstream release packaging consumes them. That policy has a separate dependency key and never changes release preparation. Selection warms versioned desktop download caches from a checksum-verified mirror before fan-out; the fast job, which starts beside selection, warms a cold one itself and builds a cold workflow linter from its pinned source. Desktop packaging retains platform-specific runtime and builder downloads independently of dependency-store keys. Pinned dependency preparation shares immutable package bytes keyed by runtime pin, dependency inputs, toolchain and platform. Each checkout keeps its own carry-modified source and workspace links. Dependency installers copy package bytes into private attempts, preventing Bun-cache hardlinks from exposing published generations to orphan writers. Locks and parent leases bind the PID to the boot identity and exact operating-system process start. Installer groups bind that same incarnation and an inherited nonce, preserving genuine children after the leader exits while rejecting recycled process and group numbers. Live PID-only legacy owners remain authoritative until their process or group exits, preserving mixed-version reader registration. Collection first rejects owners proven expired by boot or start identity, then observes only unresolved lease-bearing groups. It validates all removals before deleting anything and skips the entire pass if current ownership is unverifiable. Allocation first collects and then permits at most three pending attempts per dependency key, refusing further installs with an explicit error while those attempts remain protected. Publication, durable checkout references and collection share one lock; preparation also serializes each checkout across dependency keys. Collection removes abandoned attempts only after their parent and installer group exit. Prepare and the explicit gc:upstream command evict every unused dependency key under collection.lock, protecting registered worktree references, durable consumers, live key locks, install attempts and open files. Dependency identity excludes generated documentation manifests and preparation requires Bun 1.4.2. On macOS the pinned Bun installer uses its native clonefile backend to share global-cache blocks across dependency keys while keeping independent inodes and relative dependency links. Other platforms keep the copyfile backend. Both preserve the format-3 inode-isolation contract, so completed generations remain reusable. Scale test fixtures remove temporary data on both success and failure. Release runners dispose their temporary publication receipts after either outcome. Successful provider proofs release their scoped account fixtures; failures retain private diagnostics and report their location. Pending consumer leases bind their process incarnation; abandoned pending choices do not retain keys beyond actual installed references. Legacy pending consumers use their checkout lock as the liveness evidence. Legacy sibling install attempts inherit the parent dependency key's lock protection. Collection skips the whole pass when usage cannot be observed. Dependency-cache tests restrict worktree inventories to their temporary roots and open-file observation to captured fixture processes; injected observation failures still exercise the unchanged production refusal. Unused keys are atomically renamed out of the cache-hit namespace before their directories become writable and are removed; interrupted removals remain retired and are retried by later prepares. Unreferenced generations are collected without removing retained keys' current completed generations. Completion metadata is validated and published atomically, and existing consumers keep their generation through recovery. Source preparation applies root-owned E2E corrections after carries, while plan rendering needs no source checkout. Prepared-source verification applies those same corrections to its isolated index and the same explicit retired-test removals as declaration preparation; every other missing or unexpected source remains blocking. Zoho MCP masked-endpoint proofs preserve the original browser error before Effect wrapping and publish only closed active phases, error categories, names and source positions. Raw messages, endpoint URLs and browser events remain private. Embedded-app access proofs return only their numeric observation snapshot from the browser driver and keep assertions outside its redacted error boundary. Retained assertion evidence uses the same closed diagnostic projector for operation, phase, assertion name and bounded integer counters. Connector acceptance children atomically persist each active phase and latest successful assertion count before continuing. Parents retain a bounded output tail projected onto closed diagnostic fields while the child runs; raw output, credentials and session files stay private even after a forced stop. Outside-service OAuth fixture setup supplies one client identity and secret per service before provisioning; transient failures replay the identical credentials within the existing bounded setup budget. Returned credentials must match those inputs, and persistent failures still block setup. Outside-service identity recovery acquires its browser engine during runtime preparation; the bounded engine check launches the prepared binary without network installation. Local emulator HTTP fixtures bind the exact IPv4 loopback origin they advertise, so a competing listener cannot claim that origin. Generated renderer strings follow the pinned code generator exactly and are excluded from source formatting; their dedicated freshness check stays authoritative. Native provider product fixtures serve catalog sources through the production framework mapper. Zendesk maps both basicAuth and oauth2 declarations to the saved OAuth bearer credential; unknown schemes remain unavailable. Catalog lint and connector proofs check the served framework mapping while pinned source snapshots remain unchanged; invalid source pins and local app release mismatches still fail. Pool provisioning fixtures include the organization removal tombstone table, so app writes exercise the normal parent-lock guard. Packaged app renderer bytes and content-hashed static paths are generated together with the pinned build tool after protocol or framework changes. The pool Worker typecheck uses only platform declarations. Shared fetch telemetry preserves browser-only options when present, and redirect policy serializes URL bases before platform parsing. Personal-data reporting scenarios that use organization account APIs and authenticated actors run on hosted targets; the local product has no hosted signup or organization session fixture. Connector sign-in and tool proofs participate in affected selection and the aggregate gate, including changes to their catalog source, loopback OAuth UI and SDK protocol carries. Pull requests default to source, carry and unit checks without heavy suites. Main pushes, batch pushes, batch pull requests from forks, and other explicitly full or release-update labelled pull requests select every heavy job; a same-repository batch pull request stays fast because its branch push runs the full suite on the same head. Only a full run publishes the check status; this repository's ordinary pull requests publish check (affected), and forks and same-repository batch pull requests publish check (fast-only). The affected lane runs the fast jobs plus the heavy jobs whose inputs a changed file reaches over the repository's import graph. The fast job and its owned unit legs need no selection and start with the Check: each resolves the comparison commit with selection's own step and the lane with selection's lane function, and the aggregate requires the fast job's lane to equal selection's. The owned unit tests run in two legs and the pool runtime contracts in two parts; each test file hashes to exactly one leg or part, so together they run the same files as one run did. Job inputs are derived from the workflow steps, declared areas and scenario declarations; unresolved paths select every heavy job, and batch and main pushes still run every heavy job. A pull request's full runs and its other runs have separate concurrency groups, so a label's full run never waits behind the lane run of its head; selection cancels that lane run and any older run of an earlier head, best effort. Main pushes keep independent runs. The aggregate accepts skips only for unselected jobs and requires successful fast checks and selected jobs. Same-repository release changes additionally require the read-only production baseline probe; forks and unrelated changes skip it explicitly. The aggregate checks that a full lane selected the complete suite. Account usage owns the retained-row, row-polling and app-traffic scenarios once; other account jobs exclude those same target and runtime pairs. Host-managed account and pool-action proofs run as two selected jobs with independent unchanged deadlines, identical runtime preparation and separate retained evidence; their selectors partition the complete previous suite. Signed identity recovery and saved-client diagnostics likewise run as separate selected jobs with identical browser preparation; every scenario and step deadline stays unchanged. HITL navigation and retained account controls run in a separate required job; the target and scenario assignments partition the original suite exactly once with unchanged deadlines. Access-editor dispatch and publishing-layout proofs run in their own selected job with the same runtime preparation and evidence retention, so their independent startup costs do not consume the approval and analytics job deadline. The shared browser installer refreshes Ubuntu package indexes before its exact Playwright dependency probe; failed index refreshes and changed reports refuse. Both CI lanes require a real browser installation on the current hosted runner image. The PR fast job and local pre-push run the same prepared-source preflight: import boundaries, changed-source style, packaged app freshness, strict connector source identity and all root typecheck configurations. The authoring declarations are built before the pool typecheck; full connector runtime proofs keep their separate required jobs. Production builds independently check Worker startup limits. Profiler backoff proofs record the requested delays through an injected sleeper while ordinary measurements use the normal timer; inconclusive starts retain their bounded retry count and unknown result. Check routes declared e2e shard rows and benched named jobs (a matrix job leg by leg) to the self-hosted runners first, longest first, on the first attempt of main and batch pushes and of pull requests whose head is this repository, while the box's own heartbeat on its label (triggered by each Check's start from the default branch, or scheduled or dispatched on main) reported healthy within 10 minutes with a CI budget above 0, up to the smaller of the configured slots and that budget less the box jobs already queued or running, for rows that fit their own timeout at twice their planned minutes, with a second wave waiting on the box when it still ends by the Check's floor at 1.25 times its minutes; hosted runners take the rest. box-watch cancels a run whose box jobs the box never takes, and box-rerun re-runs them on hosted runners unless a job failed for real. Shard jobs read the label select validated, never the variable at job time. Fork pull requests and re-runs always run hosted, and any lookup failure runs every job hosted. The aggregate requires each routed shard under its job name. The oversized acquisition upload proof accepts a write-side EPIPE or ECONNRESET during dispatch or response reading only when the independent Worker observation still proves the exact byte limit, cancellation and zero backend calls. The focused app-listener proof uses native HTTP because Node fetch replaces an explicit Host header. The backend must observe the exact requested app host through the held origin. Each production-operation binding proof runs alone with its own database. Signed adoption proofs run two spec files in two test processes with independent scenario identities and identity-scoped SQL gates; every case keeps its own provisioning and cleanup within the unchanged deadlines. Scoped consumer baselines join a completed successful host last-success response and the real pool App frame setup before measuring idle routing. Accounts idle baselines also join the initial saved-account projection response and its completed body. The initial transport hold covers every competing saved-account projection until release, so another successful read cannot settle readiness early. Other reads remain unheld, the directory fallback stays visible without an Updating badge, and the idle-read assertion is unchanged. Embedded database startup for lone-commit delivery proofs completes in a separately bounded setup hook. The original five-second test still bounds delivery without another notification, and teardown remains bounded. The scope runner builds the shared framework once after installing its proof inputs, and each isolated managed run explicitly reuses that unchanged build output. The hosted fallback assigns each identity scenario its own matrix job. CI assigns each identity scenario its own matrix job so independent Cloud startup costs do not accumulate against one job deadline. A reviewed flake list names each temporary exception and its owning fix. Only final reports with explicit zero unhandled and suite errors can classify a known failure. Physical checks require an accounts-only checksum diff at the unchanged comparison; fixture removal requires a passed body before its exact teardown failure. Missing evidence and every other failure remain blocking. Removing an entry restores blocking without changing the test. Before Vitest starts, binding variants preboot original and changed Alchemy stages with the same canonical origin and configured secrets; timed fixture operations only switch the retained listener, and suite teardown owns both runtimes. App lint reads the immutable host manifest from the exact pinned Git object in its sparse source checkout and then applies manifest carries. It needs no live content API request or host installation. The shared-account health scenario runs in the hosted account gate and identifies status dots by their image role. Physical database fixtures hold runner-owned shared table locks across the clone and both account and receipt baseline reads. Physical database fixtures prepare both Postgres targets before timed scenarios; target readiness inspection is read-only. Container startup finishes before the source snapshot lock is acquired. Physical database fixtures wait for completed initial user setup and saved default-account links before cloning. PostgreSQL commit notifications wake a fresh readiness read; unrelated member work does not delay the baseline. Runner-owned shared table locks cover the clone and both account and receipt baseline reads. The lock scope rolls back before target selection and releases on failure, so normal writes resume after the consistent baseline is captured. Managed Cloud owns retained canonical API and app loopback listeners before creating fixtures. Alchemy binds their private listeners on OS-assigned ports with bind-only collision recovery; each resource's own successful startup announcement supplies its forwarding destination. Original and changed preboot runtimes own distinct app origins. Binding-change proofs for outside-service recovery switch between exactly two prebooted runtimes within the scenario and restore the original in finalization; no switch may launch a replacement. Deliberate restarts keep each canonical origin and replace only its destination. Managed Cloud owns a retained canonical loopback listener before creating fixtures. The production-operation database proxy also binds an OS-assigned loopback listener before allocating its backend and keeps it for the environment scope. Runtime bindings and fixture replies use that actual held port. Proxy startup and cleanup failures retain only closed OS codes and bounded errno values; raw exception messages and nested causes never enter diagnostics. Alchemy binds the private API on an OS-assigned port with bind-only collision recovery, and startup output supplies the actual forwarding destination. Deliberate restarts keep the canonical origin and replace only that destination. Managed Cloud failure evidence distinguishes forwarding request errors from upstream server responses and records closed transport codes and socket reuse without request data or raw exceptions. The gateway drains pending diagnostic writes when its scope closes. Identity and account-recovery evidence retains every runtime variant startup log. Managed Cloud startup retains sanitized Alchemy failure output through stream completion or a bounded quiet period before rejecting readiness. The same cause reaches cloud.log and the startup error; successful readiness has no extra wait. Compiler evidence waits on saved-line notifications through authenticated fixture control until its trace and source digest reach the requested phase, within the scenario's existing deadline. Documentation builds read licensed Latin Geist faces from repository assets through the local font provider, including normal and italic variable weight ranges. CI rebuilds with empty font caches and refuses font requests to the outside service. Live model-probe capture keeps bounded response bodies, including non-JSON failures, with credentials redacted before evidence leaves the browser. The dashboard renderer excludes browser analytics through the framework compiler's client-only boundary; browser event capture and replay keep the same synchronous entry points. The final pool image declares its OAuth completion-receipt capability so upgrading that runtime changes the published image fingerprint. Worker builds place hashed chunks below a chunks directory, leaving exactly one root entry for startup profiling regardless of hash characters. Container reconciliation checks the observed image against the retained publication digest even when input hashes match. Each production release verifies the published manifest and config bytes against their digests, reads the actual platform and OAuth completion capability label, and captures that evidence with its candidate and input fingerprint. Registry manifest and blob paths retain the literal colon in validated OCI digest references. It then verifies the application and every active instance before recording success. Missing publication evidence, failed readiness or drift stops without automatic rollback. Layout evidence queries the visible destination heading and reads its bounds in one browser task, so replacing a lazy fallback cannot leave a detached element handle between selection and measurement. Shared HTTP route declarations initialize as pure metadata factories, so the MCP entry point omits unused route descriptions while HTTP entry points retain the same public contracts. Client releases publish, once per clients version, exactly the files of a walked manual build on main as the signalbox-clients-<version> Latest release; only the job that checks out no code holds the Android release key, and the Mac update key reaches only a job that checks out the feed script alone; each walked build carries its signed Mac update feed; test builds point the Mac app at another feed and may then build every client as a higher version from the same commit; publish refuses a run whose recorded feed isn't the Latest release's or whose version differs from its commit's, treats runs that recorded no feed as production builds, and never moves a clients Latest release to an equal or lower version; published apps carry the permanent run.signalbox.client identifiers while other builds keep development ones, and the Releases page holds product releases only. The failure report module turns a release or operations command's failure into one structured error with what happened, why, the fix and a cause chain, on the error line and, where the caller asks, in the step summary; the preview lifecycle and the shared process runner use it. A cause is reduced to its type name, codes and statuses; the message of a plain error or a thrown string is kept after the strict message rules, and a library's own error classes keep their names only. A failed request is said to have sent nothing only when its connection could not be opened, collected failures take the advice of the least safe of them and claim nothing when some were not read, and a failed command among them never turns advice that a write may have landed into a rerun. A failed command keeps its last output lines for that report while its output stays live, and says whether it printed nothing, never started or was stopped; the carry replay runs its short commands with no process in between and passes their output on when each ends. A command Alchemy ran is named by its program and the words that say what it runs, never its arguments, and by how it ended, with the standard error Alchemy's error keeps; when that is empty the report says the command's standard output was not kept and to run the command alone. Every printed string loses credentials, URLs, addresses, emails and home directories and cannot issue a workflow command. An error's message also loses quoted values and every dotted name; a command's output and a refusal's own text keep file names, property paths and quoted names, and a refusal's text keeps content digests."
      metadata { constraint "Neutral workerd proof bundles resolve module and main package entry points so CommonJS dependencies remain available under the runtime compatibility flags. Owned unit lanes share the manifest test options, with a sixty-second default deadline and execution-order start and completion diagnostics. Deliberately longer installer proofs have finite ninety-second deadlines; the four sampled capacity weeks and the 48 MiB vault history are built once while their test file loads, outside any test's deadline, each test starting from its own copy, and that history's push, repack and check have a five-minute process bound; owned unit steps stop after fifteen minutes and the fast job and each unit leg after thirty, including leaked subprocess stalls."; code "e2e/zoho-people.mjs|e2e/zoho-people-fixture.mjs|e2e/clerk-sign-in.mjs|e2e/clerk-signin|e2e/workos-sign-in.mjs|e2e/management-signin|e2e/browser-hydration.test.mjs|e2e/embedded-activation.test.mjs|e2e/embedded-browser-boundaries.patch|e2e/hitl-inventory-lifetime.patch|e2e/listed-account-bindings-readiness.patch|e2e/plans|e2e/pool-removal-intent.mjs|e2e/pool-recipe-checks.mjs|e2e/recipes|.github/workflows/pool-basic-budget.yml|scripts|.upstream/e2e|e2e/automatic-account-rechecks.test.ts|e2e/account-recheck-fixture.ts|e2e/account-recheck-cooldowns.test.ts|e2e/account-recheck-dispatch.test.ts|.upstream/scripts/compiler-cache-proof|assets/fonts/geist|e2e/slack/catalog.mjs|e2e/slack/run.ts|e2e/aws|containers/pool/fetch_releases.py|containers/pool/fetch_sources.py|containers/pool/Dockerfile|.github/workflows/ci.yml|.github/workflows/ci-cache-warm.yml|.github/workflows/framework-rebuild.yml|.github/workflows/app-lint.yml|.github/workflows/clients-release.yml|.github/workflows/release-guard.yml|clients/release-notes.md|clients/test-version.sh|.upstream/scripts/e2e-prepare.ts|.upstream/scripts/e2e-check-web.ts|e2e/zoho-people-mcp.mjs|e2e/zoho-people-mcp-fixture.mjs|e2e/zoho-people-mcp-privacy.mjs|e2e/zoho-